# Managed by the foray-planner ansible role. Every scheduled foray-*.service unit
# (foray-job.service.j2) runs `docker run --pull always`, and every deploy leaves the
# previous image's digest behind - nothing removes them. That silently filled the droplet's
# disk to 99% (25G root volume, only 405M free) and broke every deploy from #350 through #354
# with "no space left on device" pulling the new image, discovered only by reading a failed
# CD run's logs rather than any alert. `docker image prune -a` only removes images with no
# container currently using them, so this can never touch what's actually running.
#
# `--filter until=24h` (a Copilot review catch, PR #355): a `docker pull` followed by
# `docker run`/container creation is two steps, not one - between them a freshly pulled image
# has zero container references and looks exactly like the stale images this exists to remove.
# Every `foray-*.service` job's `--pull always` and the deploy path's image pull both have that
# gap. Real coordination (a lock shared with every job + the deploy playbook) is more machinery
# than a housekeeping prune warrants; skipping anything pulled in the last day is enough since
# nothing legitimately stale is ever less than a day old, and docker-prune.timer.j2 also moves
# this outside the 02:00-05:00 night-job window as the other half of the fix.
[Unit]
Description=Prune unused Docker images (disk-space self-heal)
After=docker.service
Requires=docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker image prune -a -f --filter "until=24h"
